<p>A flat elevation closes a kitchen the way a black-and-white spec sheet sells a sports car. Homeowners don't buy reveals and rail widths — they buy the <em>feeling</em> of standing in the room. That's exactly why the work below is worth your attention: our dealer <strong>Studio Home</strong> built all three of these renderings in-house in <strong>Cyncly's 2020 Design</strong> — the same software you already use to lay out Shiloh and Eclipse — turning a standard CAD project into photorealistic, lighting-accurate images a client can react to before a single cabinet is ordered.</p>

<p>All three concepts are <a href="/brands/shiloh-cabinetry">Shiloh</a> and <a href="/brands/eclipse-cabinetry">Eclipse</a> cabinetry, built from real catalog products you can quote today. The kitchen, media lounge, and primary bath were <strong>designed and rendered by Megan Madden of <a href="https://www.mystudiohome.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Studio Home</a></strong> in University Place, Washington — start to finish, in 2020 Design.</p>

